        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor: extract reusable AI runtime package",
        "body": "Closes #99040\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's model protocol adapters and streaming registry were embedded in the main application, so other packages could not reuse the proven Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Google, Vertex, Mistral, and ChatGPT/Codex transports without taking on OpenClaw's application runtime.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis extracts one public package: `@openclaw/ai`. It contains the provider-neutral model/message/tool contracts, validation, diagnostics, event streams, isolated runtime registry, and lazy provider adapters. `packages/llm-core` remains a private implementation package and is bundled into `@openclaw/ai`; consumers never install or publish a second OpenClaw AI package.\n\nOpenClaw consumes `@openclaw/ai` as a normal workspace dependency, which `pnpm pack` converts to the exact release version. Runtime stream construction also resolves through `@openclaw/ai/event-stream`, preserving one `EventStream` constructor identity across OpenClaw, agent-core, and external consumers. Built-in provider registration is table-driven, reducing production TypeScript while keeping the process-default registry for existing callers.\n\nThe release workflow now packs, verifies, and publishes only `@openclaw/ai` before the root package. Workspace dependencies are omitted from pre-publication npm shrinkwrap generation because they are published beside the root package; the packed root manifest still contains the exact AI package version.\n\n## User Impact\n\nExisting OpenClaw users should see no configuration, model selection, authentication, or streaming behavior change. Installing `openclaw` installs the exact `@openclaw/ai` release automatically. Library consumers can depend on that one package, create isolated runtimes, and opt into the eight built-in API adapters.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `pnpm build` passed: package declarations, root bundle, CLI/bootstrap guards, runtime postbuild, plugin SDK export check, and Control UI build.\n- 16 focused AI/agent test files passed with 205 tests; release/version tooling added 27 passing tests.\n- Packed `@openclaw/ai@2026.6.11`, installed it alone into an external npm consumer, strict-compiled its public types/subpaths, verified shared `EventStream` identity, and registered all eight built-in providers. No `@openclaw/llm-core` package was installed.\n- Packed the root package and ran `openclaw-npm-prepublish-verify` with only the AI tarball: prepared tarball install passed.\n- `node scripts/generate-npm-shrinkwrap.mjs --check`, dependency pins, duplicate target coverage, `actionlint .github/workflows/openclaw-npm-release.yml`, docs listing, formatting, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Testbox `tbx_01kwj7b98gh3vd94v4k0bzzeb3` passed conflict, attribution, SDK boundary, duplicate coverage, dependency pin, 70-package shrinkwrap, Canvas resource, and package patch guards. Its warning-only temp-file reporter exited because the targeted Testbox checkout had no `origin/main...HEAD` merge base; hosted PR CI remains the broad gate.\n- Fresh Codex autoreview completed clean with no accepted/actionable findings.\n",
